---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import TerritorialExtentMap from '../../components/TerritorialExtentMap.astro';
import territorialExtentRaw from '../../data/maps/territorial-extent.json';
const territorialExtent = territorialExtentRaw as any;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Carthaginian territorial extent over time — Qart-Hadasht"
  description="Five-state historical map of Carthaginian territorial extent at 550, 264, 218, 202, and 146 BCE, with directly-administered, tributary, network, and lost zones distinguished visually."
>
  <article class="max-w-5xl mx-auto px-6 py-10">
    <p class="eyebrow mb-3">Historical atlas</p>
    <h1 class="font-serif text-4xl sm:text-5xl mb-4 tracking-tight">
      Carthaginian territorial extent over time
    </h1>
    <p class="text-lg text-sand-900 leading-snug mb-8 max-w-3xl">
      Five states across six centuries of Carthaginian history — from
      the network hegemony of the mid-6th century BCE through the
      reduced African polity on the eve of the 146 BCE destruction.
      The visual encoding distinguishes the nature of Carthaginian
      relationships with territory at each moment: directly administered
      hinterland, tributary or allied zone, network sphere of influence,
      or territory lost since the prior state.
    </p>

    <TerritorialExtentMap config={territorialExtent} height="600px" />

    <section class="mt-12 max-w-3xl">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl mb-3 tracking-tight">
        Why the encoding matters
      </h2>
      <p class="text-sand-900 leading-relaxed mb-4">
        Carthaginian relationships with territory were not uniform across
        time. At 550 BCE the Carthaginian polity was substantially a
        network of trading nodes and limited hinterland zones, not a
        contiguous territorial empire. At 264 BCE the polity at its
        widest reach combined directly administered African hinterland,
        tributary zones (the Emporia of Tripolitania, the inland zone
        beyond Cap Bon), and outright island holdings under direct
        Carthaginian administration (Sicily west of the Halycos,
        Sardinia, southern Corsica, the Balearics). At 218 BCE the
        Mediterranean-island holdings were lost but a substantial second
        Carthaginian polity had been built in Iberia under the Barcid
        family, with its own directly-administered core around Carthago
        Nova and a broader tributary zone extending across most of
        Iberia south of the Ebro. At 202 BCE the Iberian polity was
        gone and the African core had been reduced by Treaty-mandated
        Numidian territorial transfers. At 146 BCE fifty years of
        further Masinissa encroachment had reduced the polity to
        substantially the Cap Bon peninsula, the Medjerda lower valley,
        and the immediate coastal strip.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sand-900 leading-relaxed mb-4">
        A single visual metaphor (filled polygon) for all five dates
        would flatten these distinctions. The layered encoding here
        admits them: solid fill for directly-administered hinterland,
        diagonal hatching for tributary and allied zones, translucent
        dashed-border fill for the pre-territorial network sphere of
        the early period, and faint dashed outline for territory lost
        since the prior state.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-10 max-w-3xl">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl mb-3 tracking-tight">
        What the polygons are and aren't
      </h2>
      <p class="text-sand-900 leading-relaxed mb-4">
        The polygons are scholarly approximations derived by visual
        reference to the standard reference maps in
        Aubet's <em>The Phoenicians and the West</em> (2nd ed. 2001),
        Lancel's <em>Carthage: A History</em> (1995), Hoyos's
        <em>Hannibal's Dynasty</em> (2003) for the Barcid Iberian
        state, and MacDonald's <em>Carthage</em> (2015). They are
        not GIS-precise polity boundaries: the boundary between
        directly-administered Carthaginian territory and the Numidian
        and Libyan polities to the south and west was a gradient
        rather than a line; the boundary between "tributary" and
        "allied" relationships was administered through specific
        local arrangements rather than through a uniform
        administrative apparatus; the network nodes of 550 BCE
        represent presence and trading relationship rather than
        bordered territorial control. Treat the polygons as
        illustrative of the configuration's substantive shape
        rather than as authoritative boundary documentation.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sand-900 leading-relaxed">
        The dates themselves are conventional pivots in the
        Carthaginian historical arc: the eve of Alalia and the
        opening of contested western Mediterranean naval politics
        (550); the eve of the First Punic War (264); the eve of the
        Second Punic War (218); the eve of the Treaty of 201 (202);
        and the eve of the destruction (146). The intervening
        configurations — the post-Himera 480 retraction, the
        Agathocles-invasion crisis of 310, the moments between the
        five chosen pivots — could be added but would multiply the
        toggle complexity without changing the substantive arc the
        five states already document.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-10 max-w-3xl border-t border-sand-300 pt-6">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl mb-3 tracking-tight">Related</h2>
      <ul class="text-sand-900 space-y-2 leading-relaxed">
        <li>
          <a class="underline decoration-tyrian-500 underline-offset-2" href="/themes/phoenician-colonial-network">
            Phoenician colonial network theme
          </a>
          — the network-first framing the 550 BCE state visualizes.
        </li>
        <li>
          <a class="underline decoration-tyrian-500 underline-offset-2" href="/narratives/the-barcid-iberian-state">
            The Barcid Iberian state narrative
          </a>
          — what the 218 BCE Iberian polygons represent as substantive
          state-building.
        </li>
        <li>
          <a class="underline decoration-tyrian-500 underline-offset-2" href="/people/masinissa">
            Masinissa
          </a>
          — the agent of the 202-to-146 BCE territorial reduction.
        </li>
        <li>
          <a class="underline decoration-tyrian-500 underline-offset-2" href="/editorialTakes/masinissa-strategic-opportunist">
            Editorial take: Masinissa as strategic opportunist
          </a>
          — the site's reading of the fifty-year program.
        </li>
        <li>
          <a class="underline decoration-tyrian-500 underline-offset-2" href="/periods">
            Period overviews
          </a>
          — seven era-level syntheses that walk through the historical
          arc the map states pivot on.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
